# Personal live config (may contain secrets/local overrides) — the shipped
# canonical default config the router loads and users copy/edit is
# config.default.yaml, tracked, a separate file with its own lifecycle so
# package updates to it never collide with a user's local instance.
config.yaml

# Local working docs — plans, handovers, audits, assessments.
#
# This was six individually-named files, one rule added each time a doc landed
# in the root, and one of them (R1-HANDOFF.md) no longer existed. A directory
# ends the pattern instead of extending it: a new note needs no new rule, and
# nothing lands in the repo root by default. ASSESSMENT.md lives here for a
# reason worth keeping in view — it carries unfixed security findings and must
# stay out of public history.
notes/

# And by name as well, because the directory rule only protects a file once
# it is already in notes/. R1-HANDOFF.md was committed before a by-name rule
# was added to stop the recurrence; deleting that rule to "simplify" removed
# the guard against the exact mistake it was added for. Writing ASSESSMENT.md
# to the repo root again shows up as untracked-and-unignored, which `git add
# -A` would stage — into a PUBLIC repo, carrying unfixed security findings.
# Belt and braces costs six lines.
ASSESSMENT.md
HANDOVER.md
IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md
LAUNCH-PLAN.md
BILLING_AUTH_AUDIT.md

